WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.389
Howdy! The goal of this series of
classes is to make you fall in love with bioinformatics.

00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:12.599
Whatever you are studying
now: computer science, or biology, or

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:19.010
mathematics, we want you to consider
bioinformatics as well. Why? Because

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.359
bioinformatics is a new frontier in
all of these disciplines and because it is

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:30.330
built on all of them. I first learned about
bioinformatics when I was completing my

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:36.469
PhD in discrete mathematics a quarter
century ago. And the moment I heard about

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:41.460
this futuristic new discipline on the
interface of computing and biology, I

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:48.840
instantly switched, and never looked back. At
that time, bioinformatics was an obscure

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.370
discipline, but today it is a huge field
that is making a digital revolution

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:02.050
in biology and that opened the era of
personalized medicine. But one thing has

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:08.580
not changed: bioinformatics remains the
same frontier, the same Wild West, as it

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:15.200
was a quarter century ago; and that is
why we are wearing these crazy outfits ...

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.020
... you mean, apart from the fact that they
look good on us. Sure! In this specialization we're

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.220
going to discuss many questions that are
asked in modern biology and medicine

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:30.400
such as "Which animal gave us SARS?" or "Do
we have a clock gene?" or "How is it that

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.540
we find disease-causing mutations?" After
we ask each of these biological

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.240
questions we'll then learn about the
computational approaches that are used

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.890
to solve them, from clustering algorithms
to evolutionary tree construction to

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.240
machine learning approaches. Along the
way we're going to travel back in time

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.240
to solve a 300 year-old mathematical
problem,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.899
wander around Manhattan as tourists,
we'll even get lost in a Japanese casino

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:02.920
run by yakuza! All of these adventures
may sound like risky propositions, but we

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.189
hope that you will join us to see what
they have to do with bioinformatics.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.640
Although these instructors may appear
crazy, they are not quite as mad as they

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.379
look.

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:17.440
Dr. Pavel Pevzner is a Distinguished
Professor of computer science at the

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.879
University of California San Diego and a
leading authority on bioinformatics.

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:26.140
He's dressed this way because he sometimes
thinks that he's a sheriff of

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:31.200
bioinformatics. a frontier discipline
underpinning the digital revolution in

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.030
biology and personalized medicine. Dr.
Phillip Compeau is an assistant teaching professor

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.920
of Computational Biology at Carnegie Mellon
University. To learn why he is dressed

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.510
this way you'll need to take this course
or read the textbook,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.130
Bioinformatics Algorithms: An Active Learning Approach,
co-authored by the two speakers.

